<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n42587" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἑρμ-ᾰθήνη</span></b> , ἡ,
			<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>terminal bust</i> (cf. 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἑρμῆς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					1.2
				</span></i>
			) <i>with head of Athena,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Cic.
				<u>Att.</u>
				1.1.5
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				1.4.3
			</span></i>; so of busts with heads of other divinities, of Eros, <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἑρμ-έρως</span></b> , <i><span class="en">
				Plin.
				<u>HN</u>
				36.33
			</span></i> (pl.); of Heracles, <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἑρμ-ηρακλῆς</span></b> , <i><span class="en">
				Cic.
				<u>Att.</u>
				1.10.3
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				<u>Milet.</u>
				1(7)
			</span></i> No.<i><span class="en">
				305
			</span></i>; of Pan, cf. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἑρμόπαν.</span>
				(<i><span class="en">
				Cic.
				<u>Att.</u>
				1.4.3
			</span></i> appears to explain <u>Hermathena</u> as a Janus-like
			bust of Hermes and Athena; this is perh. a pun, but cf. 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἑρμῆς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					1.2
				</span></i>
			.)</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
